; The classic unstratifiable program — `p ← absent q; q ← absent p`.
;
; Both guards pass against the empty world, so a boundary that admitted a
; whole round's worth of candidates would derive **both** — and `{p, q}` is
; not a model of this program under any reading. S1.21.8 admits **at most one
; candidate per round** for exactly this reason: the queue is empty at
; quiescence, so the admitted candidate fires immediately, against exactly the
; world its guard was judged against, and the other candidate is re-judged
; against the world that firing made.
;
; What this fixture pins is therefore not *which* of the two is derived — that
; is the engine's own (priority, FIFO) order and it is deterministic — but that
; exactly one is, and that the second candidate is then **retired** rather than
; left waiting: its guard is monotone, so once it finds a match it finds one
; forever.
;
; The one-at-a-time discipline is a soundness requirement, not a throttle, and
; the argument for it is a fixture rather than a comment.
(relation thing Thing)
(relation p Thing)
(relation q Thing)

(rule derive-p ()
  :match  (and (thing ?x) (absent (q ?x)))
  :assert (p ?x)
  :priority 200
  :why    "no q for {?x}, so p")

(rule derive-q ()
  :match  (and (thing ?x) (absent (p ?x)))
  :assert (q ?x)
  :priority 200
  :why    "no p for {?x}, so q")

(thing one :source "the only object")
